window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][25]={"university_physics_en:M25-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M25-L1 – Reaction kinetics, activation barriers and temperature<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M25 – Bachelor: Interdisciplinary applications and scientific practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M5: thermodynamics; M13: probability\ndistributions; logarithms and elementary differential equations. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish an empirical rate\nlaw from a reaction mechanism, derive a time course and use the\nArrhenius equation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and observation<\/h2>\n<p>Why can reactions with similar energy changes proceed at very\ndifferent rates? In a constant-volume vessel, the concentration\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof a reactant is measured. The rate is not determined by the free energy\nchange alone: molecules must reach suitable configurations and often\ncross an activation barrier. Thermodynamic driving force therefore does\nnot fix the rate. Reaction order is inferred from measurements; for\nmultistep mechanisms it cannot generally be read off from stoichiometric\ncoefficients.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and equations<\/h2>\n<p>For an observed first-order rate law, separation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dc/dt=-kc<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dc/c=-k\\,dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand integration yields exponential decay. The half-life does not depend\non the initial concentration. The Arrhenius approximation represents the\ntemperature dependence of a rate constant for a fixed mechanism. It is a\nuseful local model rather than a universal exact law.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the gas constant,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nabsolute temperature,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nactivation energy and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\na prefactor. A plot of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nagainst\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas slope\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-E_a/R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis constant. The unit\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[k]=\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napplies only to first-order kinetics.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>k<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{dc}{dt}=-kc,\\qquad c(t)=c_0e^{-kt},\\qquad t_{1/2}=\\frac{\\ln 2}{k},\\qquad k(T)=A\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{E_a}{RT}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.020<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0.020\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_0=1.0\\,\\mathrm{mol\\,L^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe concentration after\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.368<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_0e^{-1}\\approx0.368\\,\\mathrm{mol\\,L^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe half-life is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>34.7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">34.7\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstays fixed and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_a=40\\,\\mathrm{kJ\\,mol^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincreases between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>300<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">300<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>310<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">310\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>300<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>310<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.68<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exp[(E_a/R)(1/300-1/310)]\\approx1.68<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure depicts only the first, modeled concentration curve.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_140_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Normalized concentration as an exponentially decreasing curve versus time, with the half-life marked.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Normalized concentration as an\nexponentially decreasing curve versus time, with the half-life\nmarked.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Original model curve for a first-order process; schematic\nscales.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c/c_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter one half-life.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_{1/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.010<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0.010\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can an exothermic reaction still be slow?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c/c_0=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.010<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>69.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_{1/2}=\\ln2/0.010\\approx69.3\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A kinetic barrier or a restricted reaction pathway can slow the\nprocess; energy release alone does not determine the rate.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The single-species model assumes constant volume and constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nReverse reactions, mass transport, catalysis and changing mechanisms\nrequire further equations. All values are illustrative.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Reaction rates, reaction order and activation barriers address\nquestions different from thermodynamic equilibrium. Exponential time\nevolution is a testable model prediction.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Material properties, band gaps\nand light–matter interaction<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-3-rate-laws\">OpenStax – Chemistry 2e, Rate Laws<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · rate laws and measured reaction orders; Subject relevance to lesson: M25-L1 – Reaction kinetics, activation barriers and temperature · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-5-collision-theory\">OpenStax – Chemistry 2e, Collision Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · activation energy and Arrhenius model; Subject relevance to lesson: M25-L1 – Reaction kinetics, activation barriers and temperature · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M25-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M25-L2 – Material properties, band gaps and light–matter interaction<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M25 – Bachelor: Interdisciplinary applications and scientific practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6: electromagnetic fields; M7:\noptics; M14: band theory and semiconductors. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Connect material properties with\nelectronic states and estimate an optical absorption threshold from a\nband gap.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and observation<\/h2>\n<p>What distinguishes a transparent insulator from a semiconductor\ndetector? Microscopic bonding and accessible electronic states determine\nhow matter responds to charge and light. In a crystal, many atomic\nstates form allowed energy bands. A schematic band diagram separates\nvalence-band states from higher conduction-band states by a forbidden\nenergy interval. A band gap is an energy, not a spatial width. Real\nmaterials also have defects, transition probabilities and\ntemperature-dependent occupancies.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and equations<\/h2>\n<p>Photon energy is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\gamma=h\\nu=hc/\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt an idealized direct band edge, photons with sufficient energy can\npromote an electron; the threshold wavelength follows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\gamma=E_g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLower-energy photons may still be absorbed through other mechanisms,\nincluding defect states, while above-threshold photons need not be\ncompletely absorbed. Indirect transitions involve momentum exchange with\nphonons. Optical response also depends on refractive index, absorption\ncoefficient, reflectance, frequency and microstructure.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\gamma=h\\nu=\\frac{hc}{\\lambda},\\qquad \\lambda_g=\\frac{hc}{E_g},\\qquad E_g=E_{\\mathrm c}-E_{\\mathrm v}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For an idealized direct gap of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_g=1.50\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nusing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1240<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">hc\\approx1240\\,\\mathrm{eV\\,nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives a threshold wavelength of about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>827<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">827\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>620<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=620\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nphoton energy is about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.00\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngreater than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis energy calculation by itself does not establish a particular\nabsorption efficiency. The band diagram shows only energy ordering and a\npossible optical transition.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_014_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic energy axis with valence and conduction bands, band gap and optical-transition arrow.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic energy axis with valence and\nconduction bands, band gap and optical-transition arrow.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Original qualitative energy sketch; not a material-specific band\nmodel.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the threshold wavelength for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_g=2.0\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What are the units of a band gap?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h\\nu&gt;E_g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot fully predict absorption?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>620<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_g\\approx620\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Energy, for example joules or electronvolts.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Transition probabilities, state occupation, momentum, defects and\nsample thickness also matter.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The single-particle band picture omits excitons and complex electron\ncorrelations. The drawn bands are not a measured spectrum or a specific\nmaterial’s band structure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Material properties connect microscopic states to measurable optical\nand electrical response; the band gap is a central parameter but not the\nonly one.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Algorithms, model cost and\nnumerical scaling<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-46-photonic-materials-and-devices-spring-2006/\">MIT OCW – Photonic Materials and Devices<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · materials and light–matter interaction (course overview); Subject relevance to lesson: M25-L2 – Material properties, band gaps and light–matter interaction · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M25-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M25-L3 – Algorithms, model cost and numerical scaling<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M25 – Bachelor: Interdisciplinary applications and scientific practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17: numerical methods; elementary\nsums and Python loops. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Justify the computational cost\nof a physical procedure as a function of problem size and verify it with\noperation counts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and observation<\/h2>\n<p>A finer simulation grid has a computational cost: doubling the number\nof points may double, quadruple or even more strongly increase runtime,\ndepending on the algorithm. Accuracy and memory consumption must be\nconsidered alongside scaling. An estimate begins by defining problem\nsize\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the dominant basic operation. It is a model of the algorithm, not a\nbenchmark measurement on specific hardware.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and equations<\/h2>\n<p>A single pass over\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeasurements performs about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nupdates and therefore has linear cost. Comparing all ordered pairs\nrequires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncomparisons. Big-O notation gives an asymptotic upper growth bound up to\nconstant factors;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstates a tight asymptotic order. For a two-dimensional grid, define\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas the total number of cells rather than silently switching to side\nlength. A numerically stable method may justifiably cost more than a\nfaster unstable approximation.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∝<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_{\\mathrm{linear}}(n)\\propto n,\\qquad T_{\\mathrm{pairs}}(n)\\propto n^2,\\qquad \\frac{T_{\\mathrm{pairs}}(2n)}{T_{\\mathrm{pairs}}(n)}=4.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1000<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeasurements, a simple accumulation needs about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1000<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1000<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nadditions, whereas an ordered all-pairs comparison needs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncomparisons. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2000<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=2000<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe counts become\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2000<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2000<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\cdot10^6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus the quadratic method costs four times as much when data size merely\ndoubles. A program can count comparisons explicitly; runtime benchmarks\ncomplement but do not replace a complexity argument. The diagram plots\nnormalized idealized operation counts.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_007_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Operation counts with linear and quadratic curves versus normalized problem size.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Operation counts with linear and\nquadratic curves versus normalized problem size.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Original scaling sketch with normalized operation counts, not a\nruntime benchmark.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">By what factor does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngrow when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntriples?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many ordered pairs including the diagonal exist for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can an\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmethod be slower than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n\\log n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor small\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A factor of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>9<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>20<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>400<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20^2=400<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Constant factors, memory access and implementation overhead may\ndominate asymptotic growth for small inputs.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Complexity is tied to a precisely specified algorithm.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalone gives neither wall-clock seconds nor conclusions about\nparallelism, memory use or numerical error.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Physical modeling needs two arguments: why the equations are\nappropriate and why the computation remains feasible for the intended\nproblem size.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Sensor signals, sampling and\nantialiasing<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-006-introduction-to-algorithms-fall-2011/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Introduction to Algorithms<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · overview of mathematical algorithms and algorithmic modeling; Subject relevance to lesson: M25-L3 – Algorithms, model cost and numerical scaling · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-006-introduction-to-algorithms-fall-2011/pages/assignments/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Assignments (asymptotic complexity)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · asymptotic complexity in coursework; Subject relevance to lesson: M25-L3 – Algorithms, model cost and numerical scaling · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M25-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M25-L4 – Sensor signals, sampling and antialiasing<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M25 – Bachelor: Interdisciplinary applications and scientific practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M4: harmonic waves; M16: measurement\ndata; M23-L7: sensor acquisition. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish an analog signal\nfrom samples and reconstruction, and calculate an alias-frequency\nexample.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and observation<\/h2>\n<p>A sensor initially produces a continuous voltage that is amplified,\nfiltered and sampled before digitization. Even a noiseless instrument\ncan display a spurious low-frequency oscillation when sampled too\nslowly. Matching discrete sample values does not determine the unique\ncontinuous signal between them without a bandwidth restriction. An\nanalog low-pass filter before the analog-to-digital converter should\nsufficiently suppress unwanted frequencies beyond the usable band.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and equations<\/h2>\n<p>For a sinusoid\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=A\\sin(2\\pi f_0t+\\phi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsamples are taken at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_n=n/f_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFrequencies differing by integer multiples of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan produce the same sampled sequence with a suitable phase. The Nyquist\nboundary\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlimits the uniquely representable positive-frequency band for ideally\nband-limited signals; a sinusoid exactly at the boundary raises phase\nand sampling issues. Real filters need a transition band and sufficient\nstopband attenuation, not merely the symbolic inequality\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s&gt;2f_{\\max}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mfrac><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x[n]=A\\sin\\!\\left(2\\pi\\frac{f_0}{f_s}n+\\phi\\right),\\qquad f_{\\mathrm N}=\\frac{f_s}{2},\\qquad f_{\\mathrm{alias}}=|f_0-mf_s|.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s=10\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_0=7\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nchoosing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives an alias frequency of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBecause\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin(2\\pi\\cdot7n/10)=-\\sin(2\\pi\\cdot3n/10)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe samples also match a phase-inverted\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-Hz\noscillation. The illustration shows the continuous\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>7<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-Hz\ncurve, sample locations separated by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.1\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand a compatible lower-frequency alternative. Adequate analog filtering\nis required to avoid ambiguity about the original frequency content.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_254_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"High-frequency analog sine wave, sample points, and phase-inverted low-frequency alias matching the samples.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">High-frequency analog sine wave, sample\npoints, and phase-inverted low-frequency alias matching the\nsamples.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Original ideal sampling diagram: 7 Hz sampled at 10 Hz aliases to\n3 Hz.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the Nyquist limit for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s=1000\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the alias frequency for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>70<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_0=70\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s=100\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must antialias filtering occur before digitization?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">500\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>70<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|70-100|=30\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">After sampling, distinct analog frequencies become indistinguishable\nfrom the samples alone, unless additional information is provided.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The alias expression assumes one ideal sinusoidal component. Real\nacquisition chains have finite roll-off, jitter, quantization noise and\nbandwidth limitations; this schematic is not a filter design.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Sampling turns continuous information into numbers and requires a\njustified bandwidth limit. Without it, the original signal cannot be\nuniquely recovered from samples.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Models, idealizations and\nscientific validity<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/res-6-007-signals-and-systems-spring-2011/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Signals and Systems<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · signal processing, sampling and filtering; Subject relevance to lesson: M25-L4 – Sensor signals, sampling and antialiasing · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/res-6-007-signals-and-systems-spring-2011/resources/lecture-16-sampling/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Signals and Systems, Lecture 16: Sampling (Spring 2011)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · explicitly listed sampling and aliasing lecture topics; Subject relevance to lesson: M25-L4 – Sensor signals, sampling and antialiasing · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M25-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M25-L5 – Models, idealizations and scientific validity<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M25 – Bachelor: Interdisciplinary applications and scientific practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M1: model building; M16: uncertainty;\nM17: numerical validation. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish idealization from\nmeasurement error and model discrepancy, and perform a simple\nresidual-based model check.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and observation<\/h2>\n<p>Why can an explicitly simplified model be scientifically useful? A\nfrictionless pendulum or a point mass deliberately leaves out features\nof the real system. The model represents a selected aspect of reality\nand can make predictions under stated conditions. It is not invalid\nmerely because its assumptions are not literally true. What matters is\ndomain of validity, acceptable error and the possibility of testing\npredictions against independent observations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and equations<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe an observed value,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>;<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x_i;\\theta)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\na model prediction and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nits standard uncertainty. A residual\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>;<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_i=y_i-f(x_i;\\theta)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeasures observation minus prediction. A normalized sum of squares\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>χ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\chi^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan quantify fit under suitable assumptions; it is neither proof of the\nmodel nor automatically a probability statement without an uncertainty\nmodel. Systematic residual patterns can indicate missing physics, while\napparently random residuals alone do not confirm all assumptions.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>;<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>χ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>N<\/mi><\/munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_i=y_i-f(x_i;\\theta),\\qquad \\chi^2=\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\left(\\frac{r_i}{\\sigma_i}\\right)^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>A model predicts\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=1,2,3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMeasurements give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>6.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=2.1;4.0;6.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\neach with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe residuals are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=(0.1;0;0.2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\chi^2=(0.1/0.2)^2+0+(0.2/0.2)^2=1.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the slope is estimated from these same data, the number of degrees of\nfreedom changes. The figure shows a straight model line and three\nslightly displaced observations.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_177_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three measurements with uncertainty bars close to a straight model line and small residuals.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three measurements with uncertainty bars\nclose to a straight model line and small residuals.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Original model-and-observation diagram with constructed\neducational data.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=5.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=5.0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How much does this residual contribute to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>χ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\chi^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Give one distinct cause of a systematic pattern in residuals.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=0.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.3/0.1)^2=9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An omitted nonlinear term, a time-dependent parameter or an\nunmodeled calibration bias.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Interpreting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>χ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\chi^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrequires a defensible error and correlation model. Models may predict\nwell while remaining underdetermined about unobserved mechanisms; no\nphilosophical claim follows from a single fit statistic.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Idealizations are explicit choices about effects omitted for a\nparticular purpose. Models remain testable when assumptions, residuals\nand domains of application are visible.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Research integrity,\ndocumentation and reproducibility<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/models-science/\">Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy – Models in Science<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · types and idealization of scientific models; Subject relevance to lesson: M25-L5 – Models, idealizations and scientific validity · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M25-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M25-L6 – Research integrity, documentation and reproducibility<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M25 – Bachelor: Interdisciplinary applications and scientific practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M16: data analysis; M18: laboratory\nrecords; M19: research data workflows. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Record data provenance and\nanalysis choices so that a result can be traced and independently\nevaluated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and observation<\/h2>\n<p>A convincing plot is not yet an auditable research result. Raw\nmeasurements, calibration, filtering, exclusions and software versions\nmust be traceable if others are to assess the findings. Good research\npractice also concerns results that contradict expectations. The DFG\ntreats documentation as a condition of reviewability; this does not\nrequire exposing personal data without safeguards or publishing every\ndataset without checking rights and obligations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and equations<\/h2>\n<p>A simple provenance chain starts with unchanged raw measurements,\nproduces a processed dataset with logged parameters and links each plot\nto that exact dataset. Checksums can reveal file changes but do not\nprove scientific correctness or authorship. Computational\nreproducibility requires specified inputs, versions, random seeds and\nprocessing steps. Independent replication is different: it may involve\nfresh measurements and new experimental conditions. Documentation and\ntransparent error reporting are part of scientific method, not merely\nadministration.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mover><mo>→<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒞<\/mi><\/mover><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mover><mo>→<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒜<\/mi><\/mover><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_{\\mathrm{raw}}\\xrightarrow{\\mathcal C}D_{\\mathrm{cal}}\\xrightarrow{\\mathcal A}R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>In a teaching example, a sensor produces ten voltage readings and a\ncalibration file specifies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=2.0\\,\\mathrm{K/V}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA raw reading of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.50\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=gU=3.00\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor later checking, neither the temperature value nor a plot alone is\nsufficient: one needs at least the original reading and unit,\ncalibration version, calculation rule, processed file and processing\ntimestamp. The figure maps this transition with documentation and review\npoints.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_244_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Data flow from immutable raw observations through calibration, processing and visualization to a reported finding with audit points.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Data flow from immutable raw observations\nthrough calibration, processing and visualization to a reported finding\nwith audit points.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Schematic documentation chain from raw data through calibration\nand processing to a reported finding.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The diagram does not represent an official DFG workflow.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=2.00\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=2.0\\,\\mathrm{K/V}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What can a matching file checksum establish, and what can it not\nestablish?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must observations that contradict expectations also be\nrecorded?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=4.00\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It supports identity of the checked file bytes, not validity of the\nexperiment.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Otherwise the data record becomes selectively biased, undermining\nreview and reproducibility.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>A file hash alone is not complete evidence of provenance. Privacy,\nrights, embargoes and discipline-specific retention obligations require\nseparate assessment; the example is not legal advice.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Documentation makes the path from observation to claim inspectable.\nReliable provenance, transparent analysis choices and justified\nexclusions are elements of good research practice.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.wissenschaftliche-integritaet.de/en/gl-12-395172\">DFG – Guideline 12: Documentation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · traceable documentation; Subject relevance to lesson: M25-L6 – Research integrity, documentation and reproducibility · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.wissenschaftliche-integritaet.de/en/code\">DFG – Code of Conduct<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · framework of good research practice; Subject relevance to lesson: M25-L6 – Research integrity, documentation and reproducibility · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
